      "notes": "* Watch Rita: position, movement, pace, dribbling, passing, and how dangerous she actually is.\n* Do NOT immediately man-mark Rita. See what happens naturally first.\n* Watch defensive shape: are Pippa–CBs–Polly staying reasonably connected?\n* Watch the far-side full-back: does she stay wide or get sucked towards the ball?\n* Watch the gap between defence and midfield.\n* Watch what happens in the first 3–5 seconds after we lose the ball.\n* Watch for the girls bunching around the ball.\n* Note whether players away from the ball stay in useful positions.\n* In Parts 1–2, assess how well the 4-3-3 shape works against a stronger team.\n* At 35’, specifically compare the 4-4-2 with the earlier shape.\n* In the 4-4-2, watch whether LM/RM stay wide and whether the two CMs separate naturally.\n* Watch whether the two strikers create problems for Alti or simply occupy the same space.\n* Watch Lyla’s movement without the ball: pressing, runs, positioning, and whether she gets isolated.\n* Compare Alice + Leontine together with Lyla playing as the lone striker.\n* Watch Victoria: is she connecting the team or following the ball?\n* Watch Isi at DM, then compare her effectiveness after moving to CM.\n* Watch Martha S beyond saves: communication, distribution, decisions, and confidence.\n* Don’t panic and change things just because Alti cause problems. Let the problems reveal themselves.\n* Record quick + / 0 / − for each period: defensive shape, bunching, midfield control, attacking threat.\n* At half-time, note the 2–3 biggest problems rather than trying to fix everything.\n* After the game: identify what worked, what broke down, and what the 4-4-2 changed.\n* Most important: find out exactly how good Alti are and what makes them difficult."
    },
